    detailedNotes: 'Projectile motion is the motion of an object thrown into the air under the influence of gravity alone. It is analysed by resolving the motion into independent horizontal and vertical components. The horizontal component has constant velocity (no horizontal acceleration, ignoring air resistance). The vertical component has constant downward acceleration equal to g. Key equations: Time of flight T = 2u·sinθ/g; Maximum height H = u²sin²θ/(2g); Range R = u²sin(2θ)/g. The trajectory is parabolic. Maximum range is achieved at 45°. Complementary angles (e.g., 30° and 60°) give the same range. The motion confirms Newton\'s First Law — horizontal velocity remains unchanged without a horizontal force.',
    vivaQuestions: [
      { q: 'What is a projectile?', a: 'A projectile is any object thrown into the air with an initial velocity, whose motion is influenced only by gravity (and air resistance, in real cases).' },
      { q: 'What is the shape of the trajectory of a projectile?', a: 'The trajectory of a projectile (ignoring air resistance) is a parabola.' },
      { q: 'What is the formula for the maximum height attained by a projectile?', a: 'H = u²sin²θ/(2g), where u is initial velocity, θ is angle of projection, and g is acceleration due to gravity.' },
      { q: 'What is the formula for the time of flight of a projectile?', a: 'T = 2u·sinθ/g' },
      { q: 'What is the formula for the range of a projectile?', a: 'R = u²sin(2θ)/g' },
      { q: 'At what angle is the range maximum?', a: 'The range is maximum at 45° because sin(90°) = 1 gives the maximum value of sin(2θ).' },
      { q: 'What are complementary angles in projectile motion?', a: 'Two angles that add up to 90° (e.g., 30° and 60°) are complementary. Projectiles launched at complementary angles with the same speed have the same range.' },
      { q: 'What is the horizontal component of velocity at the highest point?', a: 'At the highest point, the vertical component is zero, but the horizontal component remains u cosθ (constant throughout the flight).' },
      { q: 'What is the horizontal acceleration of a projectile?', a: 'Ignoring air resistance, the horizontal acceleration is zero — horizontal velocity remains constant.' },
      { q: 'What is the vertical acceleration of a projectile?', a: 'The vertical acceleration is constant and equal to g = 9.8 m/s² downward (on Earth).' },
      { q: 'How do the horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile relate?', a: 'The horizontal and vertical motions are independent of each other. Time is the common parameter linking them.' },
      { q: 'What happens to the range if the launch velocity is doubled?', a: 'The range becomes four times (R ∝ u²), since R = u²sin(2θ)/g.' },
      { q: 'What is the angle of projection for minimum range (excluding zero)?', a: 'For very small or very large angles approaching 0° or 90°, the range approaches zero. At 90°, the projectile goes straight up and down.' },
      { q: 'Why does a projectile follow a parabolic path?', a: 'The constant horizontal velocity combined with the constant vertical acceleration (due to gravity) results in a parabolic trajectory.' },
      { q: 'What is the effect of air resistance on projectile motion?', a: 'Air resistance reduces the range and maximum height. The trajectory is no longer a perfect parabola — it becomes asymmetrical with a steeper descent.' },
      { q: 'What is the velocity of a projectile at the highest point?', a: 'At the highest point, the velocity equals the horizontal component u cosθ, since the vertical component is zero.' },
      { q: 'Two projectiles are launched at 30° and 60° with the same speed. Compare their ranges.', a: 'They have the same range because 30° and 60° are complementary angles (add up to 90°), so sin(60°) = sin(120°).' },
      { q: 'What are some real-life examples of projectile motion?', a: 'A javelin throw, a cannonball fired from a cannon, a basketball shot, a long jumper, and water from a fountain.' },
      { q: 'What is the path of a projectile launched horizontally?', a: 'A projectile launched horizontally follows a parabolic path downward, with constant horizontal velocity and increasing vertical velocity due to gravity.' },
      { q: 'How does the value of g on different planets affect projectile motion?', a: 'On planets with lower gravity (e.g., Moon), the range and time of flight increase for the same launch speed. On planets with higher gravity (e.g., Jupiter), range and flight time decrease.' },
    ]
